[gd_scene load_steps=2 format=3 uid="uid://btilecursormnt7"]

; The HOST's tile cursor — ADR-0207 dec. 5. A Godot INHERITED SCENE whose base is the
; addon's cursor scene, so the host owns the cursor NODE while the addon keeps the
; implementation. The third declared mount, after ADR-0204's camera and ADR-0207 dec. 1's
; map composer.
;
; 🔴 WHY A MOUNT AT ALL. ADR-0206 dec. 2 deliberately left `$TileCursor` AUTHORED IN THE
; SCENE: a node path is a scene-tree coupling, not a type reference, so publishing
; `CursorRig` closed criterion 1 without touching these. The host legitimately owns a
; cursor node; what it must not own is the addon's file path. Seven files named that path
; — three host scenes by `ext_resource` and four tests by `preload`/`load` — and now one
; does.
;
; 🔴 INHERITED, NOT INSTANCED, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE FROM THE COMPOSER. ADR-0207
; dec. 2 rejected inheritance for `ProceduralMap.tscn` on a measurement: nothing added a
; child and the composer node had no internal structure, so inheritance bought nothing and
; cost a file existing only to be inherited. The cursor's base scene DOES have internal
; structure — a `HighlightMesh` child the script drives — and an instance-wrapper would
; either flatten it or bury it one level deeper, moving every `TileCursor/...` path. An
; inherited scene keeps the root's name, type and children byte-identical, which is
; ADR-0204's stated reason and the reason dec. 5 rules this the camera's form.
;
; 🔴 NAMED `CombatCursor`, NOT `TileCursor`, AND THE GUARD IS WHY. The moved file's
; ORIGINAL host path was `assets/scenes/TileCursor.tscn`, and that path is row 3 of
; docs/EXTRACTION-3-MOVE-MANIFEST.tsv. `check_move_manifest.py` arm 1 asserts every row is
; in exactly ONE of its two places; a mount re-created at the src path makes the row read
; BOTH src and dst exist (copied, not moved) — measured, it reds. The mount is not the
; moved file and should not wear its name. `assets/scenes/CombatCamera.tscn` dodged the
; same collision by accident (the addon file it fronts is `PlayerCamera.tscn`); this one
; is deliberate, and the two mounts now read as a pair.
;
; ⚠️ IT ADDS NOTHING TODAY, deliberately, and that is not the same as being empty. The
; camera mount had a child to carry; this one exists so the seam is host-owned BEFORE a
; host has something to hang there. Overriding a base property or adding a child is a
; one-line edit here instead of a three-scene edit.

